      ["What is the fundamental concept of the 'Time Value of Money' in engineering economics?", "A dollar today is worth more than a dollar in the future because of its earning capacity (interest and investment potential)", "Money loses its value at night", "Future money is always more valuable than present money", "Time and money are identical physical units", "Money available today can be invested to earn interest, making present capital inherently more valuable than identical nominal sums received later."],
      ["What is the difference between Simple Interest and Compound Interest?", "Simple interest is calculated solely on the original principal; compound interest is calculated on both principal and accumulated interest from previous periods", "Compound interest is always lower", "Simple interest is illegal in banking", "There is no difference over 10 years", "Compound interest accelerates capital growth exponentially because accrued interest itself generates interest in subsequent compounding cycles."],
      ["What is the formula for the Future Worth F of a present sum P invested at annual compound interest rate i for n years?", "F = P * (1 + i)^n", "F = P * (1 + i * n)", "F = P / (1 + i)^n", "F = P * i * n", "Future Worth under compound interest is F = P(F/P, i, n) = P (1 + i)^n."],
      ["In an engineering Cash Flow Diagram (CFD), how are revenues (inflows) and disbursements (outflows) represented?", "Inflows are upward arrows (+); Outflows are downward arrows (-)", "Inflows are downward; Outflows are upward", "Both are horizontal arrows", "Revenues are red circles", "Cash flow diagrams represent time along a horizontal axis, plotting receipts as positive upward vectors and disbursements as negative downward vectors."],
      ["What does the 'Single Payment Present Worth Factor' (P/F, i, n) represent?", "P = F * (1 + i)^(-n)", "P = F * (1 + i)^n", "P = F / i", "P = F * n * i", "Discounting a future sum F back to present worth P uses the factor (1 + i)^(-n)."],
      ["What is the 'Minimum Attractive Rate of Return' (MARR) in engineering project evaluation?", "The lowest rate of return that an organization will accept before investing in an engineering project", "The legal bank interest rate", "The inflation rate plus 10%", "The tax rate", "MARR represents the opportunity hurdle rate of capital; projects yielding returns below MARR are economically rejected."],
      ["What does the 'Uniform Series Capital Recovery Factor' (A/P, i, n) calculate?", "The equivalent uniform annual cash flow A required to recover an initial investment P over n periods at interest i", "The future worth of an annuity", "The present worth of a gradient", "The internal rate of return", "Capital recovery converts present capital expenditure into equivalent uniform annual installments: A = P [i(1+i)^n / ((1+i)^n - 1)]."]
